# Why insured people can look sicker: selection bias, decomposed
subtitle: "Why a raw insured-minus-uninsured gap is an effect plus a selection term, which way that term runs, and why controls rarely remove it."
tags: ["selection bias","causal inference","health insurance","potential outcomes","observational studies"]
teaches: ["concepts/selection-bias","methods/potential-outcomes"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}
next: {"title":"The Oregon lottery: what randomization actually buys you","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/the-oregon-lottery-what-randomization-actually-buys-you.cast"}

## Fewer good days with coverage?
voice: male
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Insured
      - Uninsured
    values:
      - 18
      - 22
    colors:
      - '#2f6b8f'
      - '#b5482e'
    value_labels: true
    label_size: 34
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 30
    y_label: good days of 30
    box:
      x: 100
      'y': 125
      w: 440
      h: 505
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein, Taubman, Wright, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Allen, Baicker and the Oregon Health Study Group, Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2012
      finding: 'Table 13: BRFSS 2004-2009, adults 19-64 below poverty: insured minus uninsured days of good physical health in last 30, raw -2.3 (se 0.19), with demographic controls -0.9 (se 0.18). Table 9 (lottery IV, Oregon, about a year after the lottery): days of good physical health +1.3 (se 0.56).'

    scratch real cites ["finkelstein2012"] work ["Poor US adults, 2004–09","Insured vs uninsured:","−2.3 good days a month"] x 500 y 380 font_size 40 hidden true
    text town "A made-up town" #8f887c x 330 y 660 font_size 34 hidden true
    math m1 tex "{E[Y_i \\mid D_i=1]} = 18" size 44 colors {"{E[Y_i \\mid D_i=1]}":"#2f6b8f"} x 790 y 540 hidden true
    math m2 tex "{E[Y_i \\mid D_i=0]} = 22" size 44 colors {"{E[Y_i \\mid D_i=0]}":"#b5482e"} x 790 y 450 hidden true
    path m_rule #3d3833 points [[600,400],[980,400]] style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    math m3 tex "18 - 22 = -4" size 50 x 790 y 340 hidden true
    text mix1 "what coverage does" #8a5fa8 x 790 y 220 font_size 38 hidden true
    text mix2 "+ who chose it" #3d3833 x 790 y 165 font_size 38 hidden true
    mark m3_box around m3 kind box hidden true

    card title "Fewer good days with coverage?"

Why would poor adults with health insurance report fewer healthy days than poor adults without it?
    draw real_box real_line_1

In American surveys o (@draw real_line_2@)f adults below the poverty line, from 2004 to 2009, the insured  (@draw real_line_3@)had about two fewer days of good physical health a month.

Read literally, that says coverage makes poor people sicker.
    highlight real_line_3

Hardly anyone believes that, so the gap must be made of something else.
    erase real

Here we take it apart, piece by piece, and ask which way each piece pushes.
    draw axes

So picture a made-up town of low-income adults, with numbers we can open up.
    draw town

Those who stayed uninsured report twenty-two good days out of the last thirty.
    draw bar_2

Those who signed up for coverage report eighteen.
    draw bar_1

The quick comparison takes the average among the insured,
    draw m1

subtracts the average among the uninsured,
    draw m2

and calls the difference, minus four days, the effect of coverage.
    draw m_rule m3 m3_box

But each average is taken over a different set of people, and those people chose their group.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

And who signs up for coverage? Mostly the people who expect to need it.
    highlight bar_1 effect circle

    quiz "Suppose coverage did nothing at all, for anyone. What would the quick comparison show in this town?" intro "A quick check."
        right: Still negative: the people who signed up were sicker to begin with, so their bar sits low whatever coverage does.
        wrong: With no effect at all, who is in the insured bar?
        * About zero
        + Still a negative gap
        * A positive gap

So the minus four mixes two things: what coverage does,
    draw mix1

and who chose it.
    draw mix2

Splitting them takes one number that nobody ever gets to see.
    highlight m3

## The bar nobody sees
voice: male
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Insured
      - Same, without
      - Uninsured
    values:
      - 18
      - 15
      - 22
    colors:
      - '#2f6b8f'
      - '#d0865f'
      - '#b5482e'
    value_labels: true
    label_size: 30
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 30
    y_label: good days of 30
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 80
      w: 380
      h: 560

    text q2 "?" #8f887c x 270 y 300 font_size 90 hidden true
    math eq0 tex "{E[Y_i \\mid D_i=1]} - {E[Y_i \\mid D_i=0]} = -4" size 34 colors {"{E[Y_i \\mid D_i=1]}":"#2f6b8f","{E[Y_i \\mid D_i=0]}":"#b5482e"} x 760 y 620 hidden true
    math t1 tex "{E[Y_i(1) \\mid D_i=1]} - {E[Y_i(0) \\mid D_i=1]}" size 34 colors {"{E[Y_i(1) \\mid D_i=1]}":"#2f6b8f","{E[Y_i(0) \\mid D_i=1]}":"#d0865f"} x 760 y 535 hidden true
    math n1 tex "{18 - 15 = 3}" size 40 colors {"{18 - 15 = 3}":"#8a5fa8"} x 760 y 475 hidden true
    text att "ATT" #8a5fa8 x 930 y 475 font_size 34 hidden true
    text n1_name "effect on the insured" #8a5fa8 x 760 y 428 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "The bar nobody sees"

The missing number is how the people who signed up would have done without coverage.
    draw axes bar_1 bar_3

It belongs between the two bars we have, and nobody can  (@draw q2@)measure its height: those people did sign up.
    highlight bar_1 bar_3

In our town, say, coverage adds three good days to the people who took it.
    highlight bar_1

    erase q2

    ask "Coverage added three good days for them. How many would the insured have had without it? Set the middle bar." intro "Your turn first." on bar_2 store g tolerance 0.04 default 20
        right: Yes: eighteen minus three, so fifteen good days, fewer than the uninsured's twenty-two.
        wrong: You said {g:0}. Take the three days away from their eighteen: fifteen.

Fifteen: without coverage, they would have been the least healthy group of all.
    highlight bar_2 effect circle

Now the quick comparison can be split at that middle bar.
    draw eq0

Take the insured wit (@highlight bar_1 bar_2@)h coverage, minus the same people without it.
    draw t1

Eighteen minus fifteen: three days, the effect of coverage on the people who took it. Economists call it the average treatment  (@draw att@)effect on the treated.
    draw n1 n1_name

Then add the insured wi (@highlight bar_2 bar_3@)thout coverage, minus the uninsured without coverage.
    math plus tex + size 44 x 760 y 372
    math t2 tex "{E[Y_i(0) \\mid D_i=1]} - {E[Y_i(0) \\mid D_i=0]}" size 34 colors {"{E[Y_i(0) \\mid D_i=1]}":"#d0865f","{E[Y_i(0) \\mid D_i=0]}":"#b5482e"} x 760 y 310

Fifteen minus twenty-two: minus seven.
    math n2 tex "15 - 22 = -7" size 40 x 760 y 250

Both halves of that second term are lives without coverage, so insurance is not in it at all.
    highlight t2

It measures only how the two groups of people differ. That is selection bias.
    text n2_name "selection bias" x 760 y 203 font_size 32
    mark n2_box around n2 n2_name kind box

Effect plus bias: three plus minus seven gives back the minus four we started with.
    morph eq0 tex "-4 = 3 + (-7)"

So the quick comparison did its arithmetic right. It just answers a mixed question, and the bias here is more than twice the effect.
    highlight eq0

And no data set tells us how big the second term is, because one half of it is the bar nobody sees.
    highlight bar_2 effect circle

## Which way the bias runs
voice: male
domain: {"x":[-10,10],"y":[-8,14]}
vars: {"b":{"value":-7,"min":-10,"max":10},"gap":{"expr":"3 + b"}}
sources:
    - id: angrist2015
      title: 'Mastering ''Metrics: The Path from Cause to Effect (Table 1.1, NHIS 2009)'
      authors: Angrist and Pischke, Princeton University Press
      year: 2015
      finding: In the 2009 National Health Interview Survey, married adults with health insurance report better health than the uninsured, and also have about three more years of schooling, higher employment and more than twice the family income.
    - id: jackson2006
      title: Evidence of bias in estimates of influenza vaccine effectiveness in seniors
      authors: Jackson, Jackson, Nelson, Neuzil and Weiss, International Journal of Epidemiology
      year: 2006
      doi: 10.1093/ije/dyi274
      finding: '72,527 people aged 65+ in a Washington State health plan over 8 years: relative risk of death, vaccinated vs unvaccinated, 0.39 before the influenza season, 0.56 during, 0.74 after; adjusting for diagnosis codes did not remove the bias.'

    axes axes x_label "" y_label "" ticks false hidden true
    text xl "selection bias" at.data [7.2,-1.6] font_size 34 hidden true
    text yl "gap shown" at.data [2.4,13.2] font_size 34 hidden true
    text tk7 "−7" #8f887c at.data [-7.8,-1.1] font_size 30 hidden true
    text tk3 "−3" #8f887c at.data [-3.4,-1.1] font_size 30 hidden true
    text tk0 "0" #8f887c at.data [0.5,-1.1] font_size 30 hidden true
    curve true_c #8a5fa8 expr "3 + 0*x" style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    label lab_true "true effect +3" below #8a5fa8 attach_to true_c font_size 34 hidden true
    curve naive_c #3d3833 expr "3 + x" style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    label lab_naive "effect + bias" left attach_to naive_c font_size 34 hidden true
    dot town #3d3833 at.on naive_c at.x -7 guides true bind {"at.x":"b"} drag b hidden true
    math readout tex "3 + ({b:0}) = {gap:0}" size 44 x 250 y 620 hidden true
    text wrong_sign "wrong sign" #b5482e at.data [-4.3,-6.5] font_size 36 hidden true
    scratch job left cites ["angrist2015"] work ["US couples, 2009","Insured:","+3 years of school","over 2× the income"] font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch flu left cites ["jackson2006"] work ["Seniors, before flu season:","vaccinated die","less than half as often"] font_size 30 hidden true
    text pos "bias > 0" #3d3833 at.data [6,-4] font_size 38 hidden true
    mark pos_ring around pos kind box hidden true

    card title "Which way the bias runs"

So whether a raw gap flatters insurance or slanders it depends on the sign of that second term.
    draw axes xl yl tk7 tk3 tk0

Across,  (@highlight xl@)the selection bias in good days; up, the (@highlight yl@) gap the quick comparison shows.

The true effect stays at three, whatever the selection.
    draw true_c lab_true

The quick comparison is that three plus the bias, so it climbs one for one.
    draw naive_c lab_naive

Our town sits at minus seven, which gives the minus four.
    draw town readout

Anywhere left of minus three, the comparison gets even the si (@highlight readout@)gn wrong: the bias outweighs the effect.
    draw wrong_sign

Grab the point and pull it to the right, toward zero and past it, and watch where the quick comparison crosses the axis.
    explore params ["b"] watch "Watch the point slide toward zero and past it, and see where the quick comparison crosses the axis."

At minus three  (@erase wrong_sign@)the bias cancels the effect, and the comparison reads zero.
    animate b -3 duration 2.5

Between minus three and zero the sign comes out right, but the effect is understated.
    animate b -1.5 duration 2

Right of zero, the bias adds to the effect, and the comparison overstates it.
    animate b 5 duration 2.5

Where a real comparison sits depends on who chose coverage, and why.
    highlight town effect circle

Medicaid is taken up mostly by people who are sick, or expect to be, so the insured would have been less he (@draw wrong_sign@)althy anyway: the bias is negative.
    animate b -7 duration 2

Among better-off families, coverage of any kind o (@draw pos pos_ring@)ften sorts people the other way.
    erase readout town true_c lab_true wrong_sign

For instance, in a 2009 national survey of married couples, those with any insurance rated their health higher than the uninsured did.
    draw job_box job_line_1 job_line_2

They als (@draw job_line_3@)o had about three more years of schooling (@draw job_line_4@), and more than twice the income.

People like that would have been healthier without coverage too, so there the b (@highlight pos@)ias is positive, and the raw gap flatters insurance.
    erase job

    quiz "Older people who get a flu shot die less often over the winter than those who don't. Which way does selection push that comparison?" intro "A new case."
        right: In its favour: the frail and housebound skip the shot more often, so the vaccinated would have died less anyway.
        wrong: Think about who manages to get to a clinic in the autumn, and who doesn't.
        * Against the vaccine
        * No direction: deaths are random
        + In the vaccine's favour

There is even a tell. In a Washington State study of seventy thousand seniors, the vaccina (@draw flu_line_2@)ted were less than half as likely to die (@draw flu_line_3@) before the flu season had begun.
    draw flu_box flu_line_1

A vaccine cannot protect against a flu that has not arrived, so that whole gap is who chose the shot.
    highlight flu_line_3

## Why controls don't close it
