sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein, Taubman, Wright, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Allen, Baicker and the Oregon Health Study Group, Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2012
      finding: 'Table 13: BRFSS 2004-2009, adults 19-64 below poverty: insured minus uninsured days of good physical health in last 30, raw -2.3 (se 0.19), with demographic controls -0.9 (se 0.18). Table 9 (lottery IV, Oregon, about a year after the lottery): days of good physical health +1.3 (se 0.56).'
takeaways:
    - Raw gap = effect + selection bias
    - Who chooses sets the bias's sign
    - Controls remove only what's recorded

