    shape lot_sq #8a5fa8 cites ["finkelstein2012"] shape rect x 851.7 y 300 width 30 height 30 style.fill #8a5fa8 hidden true
    text lot_val "+1.3" #8a5fa8 cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 851.7 y 350 font_size 40 hidden true
    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["axis","tick_0","tick_2","tick_4","axis_lab","zero","raw_name","raw_ci","raw_sq","raw_val","ctl_name","ctl_ci","ctl_sq","ctl_val","lot_name","lot_ci","lot_sq","lot_val"] hidden true

    card title "Why controls don't close it"

The usual cure for selection bias is to compare only people who are alike: the s (@draw f1@)ame age, the same income, the same recorded diagnoses.
    draw axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

Then the bias is taken inside each group of lo (@highlight f1 part X_i effect underline@)ok-alikes, X for what was recorded, and every difference the data can see drops out.
    draw f1_lab

But people sign up for reasons no survey writes down: a c (@highlight why effect underline@)ough that won't go away, a lump, a bad winter.
    draw why

Inside every group of look-alikes, those reasons still steer the sicker into (@highlight bar_1 bar_2@) coverage, so the bias shrinks but rarely vanishes.
    draw unrec

In our town the bias was minus seven. Say the controls swap the uninsured for look-alik (@animate stage 1 duration 2@)es who report only eighteen good days: the bias (@morph f2 tex "3 + (-3) = 0"@) shrinks to minus three.
    draw f2

Then the controlled comparison reads eighteen minus eighteen: zero, and coverage looks useless.
    highlight bar_1 bar_3

    quiz "A researcher adds controls, and the insured-minus-uninsured gap moves from minus four to zero. What has she learned?" intro "A quick check."
        right: Only that: the shift shows how much the recorded differences mattered, not how much the unrecorded ones still do.
        wrong: Did the cough and the lump go into her regression?
        * That coverage has no effect
        + Only that the recorded differences mattered
        * That all the bias is now gone

So we need to check the controls against an estimate we can trust, and Oregon's study team made that comparison.
    erase axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 f1 f1_lab why unrec f2

Each row is a gap in good days of physical health a month, insured minus uninsured; the dashed line is no difference.
    draw axis tick_0 tick_2 tick_4 axis_lab zero

In national surveys of poor American adults, the insured had about two point three fewer good days.
    draw raw_name raw_ci raw_sq raw_val

Controlling for age, sex, race, education, employment and income cut that to about one fewer.
    draw ctl_name ctl_ci ctl_sq ctl_val

But in Oregon, for the people the lottery moved onto coverage, it added about one and a third good days, give or take about one.
    draw lot_name lot_ci lot_sq lot_val

The rows do not describe the same people: Oregon's lottery entrants about a year on, against poor adults across the country, so the match is rough.
    highlight raw_name ctl_name lot_name

If the national effect is near Oregon's, the controls moved the estimate only about a third of the way, and left it on the wrong side of zero.
    highlight ctl_sq ctl_val zero

So why can insured people look sicker? Because among the poor, the people who choose coverage would have been sicker without it, and that term outweighs what coverage does.
    highlight lot_sq lot_ci

    clear

A raw gap is two terms: what the treatment does, plus how the people who chose it differ.
    draw takeaway_1

Who chooses decides which way the second term pushes, and it can be big enough to flip the answer.
    draw takeaway_2

And controls remove only the differences someone recorded, which is why a lottery, blind to everything about people, is worth so much.
    draw takeaway_3

Every method in causal inference is a way of borrowing that one number nobody sees: how the treated would have done without it.
    highlight takeaway_1 takeaway_2 takeaway_3

